# /etc/inittab init(8) configuration for BusyBox
#
# Format for each entry: <id>:<runlevels>:<action>:<process>
#
# <id>: WARNING: This field has a non-traditional meaning for BusyBox init!
#	The id field is used by BusyBox init to specify the controlling tty for
#	the specified process to run on.  The contents of this field are
#	appended to "/dev/" and used as-is.
#
# <action>: Valid actions include: sysinit, wait, once, respawn, askfirst,
#                                  shutdown, restart and ctrlaltdel.

::sysinit:/etc/init.d/rcS

# Start an "askfirst" shell on the console (serial ttyS0)
::askfirst:-/bin/sh

# NO getty ON THE FRAMEBUFFER CONSOLES -- ON PURPOSE.
#
# MiSTer owns them. With fb_terminal=1 (see media/fat/MiSTer.ini), running a
# script from the OSD makes Main_MiSTer switch to tty2 and fork its own login
# program there (Main_MiSTer_ch96/menu.cpp, MENU_SCRIPTS_FB):
#
#     video_chvt(2);
#     video_fb_enable(1);
#     execl("/sbin/agetty", "/sbin/agetty", "-a", "root", "-l", "/tmp/script",
#           "--nohostname", "-L", "tty2", "linux", NULL);
#
# An init-managed shell or getty on the same tty fights it for the terminal:
# the two take turns reading the keyboard and the script output is unreadable
# or the tty is stolen back mid-run. A respawning getty on tty1 does the same
# to the framebuffer console MiSTer draws on.
#
# A stock MiSTer settles this the same way -- its inittab has exactly one tty
# line, on the serial port, and nothing on tty1..tty6.
#
# The lines are kept here, commented, because they are the first thing to
# restore when debugging without a serial cable.
#
#tty2::askfirst:-/bin/sh
#tty3::askfirst:-/bin/sh
#tty4::askfirst:-/bin/sh
#tty1::respawn:/sbin/getty 38400 tty1
#tty5::respawn:/sbin/getty 38400 tty5
#tty6::respawn:/sbin/getty 38400 tty6

# Stuff to do when restarting the init process
::restart:/sbin/init

# Stuff to do before rebooting
::ctrlaltdel:/sbin/reboot
::shutdown:/bin/umount -a -r
::shutdown:/sbin/swapoff -a
